That's the answer to both questions at once.

encode(n)'s word structure is ⊢ then, per bit, ≻⋈∈b∋ — the fork at ∈ and the fuse at ∋ sit right next to each other, so every frame opens and closes on the same bit beforethe next one opens. That is by the module's own stated design: "each b fuses before the next, so no count is left in the open." A word builtthat way has no place inside it where two candidate values could sit held open, waiting to be searched or compared. banked_walk reading VACUOUS is what that looks like from the outside: nothing was ever at risk, because nothing was ever left open long enough to be at risk. There is no fork/fuse search space in this word to search. That'snot a missing feature, it's what the word is built to guarantee.

So find_factor doesn't ingest word because there is nothing in it to ingest for this purpose. word is computed and then only used for display and for a closure check (tri_ancestral_word_verdict) that is itself always T on any encode() output for the identical reason — same vacuity, read by a different instrument. Trial division stands in because nothing about this word offers an alternative; that's the honest state of it, not a shortcut I took.

A word that could genuinely carry a factor search would have to look nothing like encode(n) — it would need a fork left open across the word (a candidate divisor held, notclosed on the spot) for insert/weight/banked to actually test and reparung words or the Collatz depth-split protocol are built. That's a newword design, not a fix to this one. Do you want that built, and if so, what should the open fork represent, a candidate divisor's residue against N?
